# Getting started

This workflow creates content inside an existing Contentful space. The provider does not create or delete spaces. Before starting, have a Contentful Management API token and the ID of an existing space and environment that the token can manage.

## Configure the provider

Set your Contentful Management API token in `CONTENTFUL_MANAGEMENT_ACCESS_TOKEN` before running Terraform. The [provider reference](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/cysp/contentful/latest/docs#authentication) explains authentication precedence and API URL configuration.

## Manage an object in an existing space

Save this complete configuration as `main.tf`. It creates a private Contentful Tag and declares the space and environment inputs. Use an existing environment the token can manage; change the default if its ID is not `master`. The Tag ID `terraform-example` must be unused in that environment; import an existing Tag instead of attempting to create it again.

For production use, add an intentional provider version constraint that matches your upgrade policy rather than relying on an unconstrained latest version.

{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

Initialize Terraform and review the first plan:

```shell
terraform init
terraform plan -var='contentful_space_id=SPACE_ID'
```

With the required inputs, the first plan proposes creating one private `contentful_tag.example` resource. Planning a new Tag does not verify that the space and environment exist or that the token can manage them; those checks happen when Contentful receives the create request. Review the plan before applying it; `terraform apply` would create that Tag in Contentful.

For existing objects that should be brought under Terraform rather than created, use the Import section of the corresponding resource page. For operation deadline and readiness behavior, see [Operation timeouts](operation-timeouts).
